std.sort — In-place sorting for slices
======================================

The `std.sort` module sorts slices in place via comparison-based
algorithms. Three implementations are exposed; pick by trade-off:

| Function           | Complexity           | Stable? | Extra memory | Use when |
|--------------------|----------------------|---------|--------------|----------|
| `sort_by`          | $O(n \log n)$ avg & worst | no | $O(\log n)$ stack | **default** — introsort |
| `quick_sort_by`    | $O(n \log n)$ avg, $O(n^2)$ worst | no | $O(\log n)$ stack | known-friendly inputs, fastest avg case |
| `heap_sort_by`     | $O(n \log n)$ worst   | no | none — no recursion | hard real-time, embedded |
| `stable_sort_by`   | $O(n \log n)$ worst   | yes | $O(n)$ heap | preserve equal-key order |
| `stable_sort_buf_by` | $O(n \log n)$ worst | yes | caller-supplied $O(n)$ | embedded / no allocator |

Comparators take two values of the element type and return a
three-way `int`: negative for `a < b`, zero for `a == b`, positive
for `a > b`. The standard `cmp_int` and `cmp_i64` helpers are
provided; for descending order use `cmp_desc(a, b) = b - a`.

Dependencies: none.

    module std.sort


`sort_by` — introsort (default)
-------------------------------

`sort_by(s, cmp)` sorts `s` in place using introsort. This is the
recommended general-purpose sort: quicksort's average speed with
heapsort's worst-case guarantee. See "Introsort" below for the
algorithm.

    sort_by[T](s: []T, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        intro_sort_by(s, cmp)


Heapsort
--------

Heapsort puts the array into a max-heap, then repeatedly extracts the
maximum into the back of the array. It runs in $O(n \log n)$ worst
case using only $O(1)$ extra memory and no recursion — both desirable
when the call stack is tight or the schedule is adversarial. The
trade-off is a 2–3× constant-factor slowdown versus quicksort on
average inputs because the swap pattern thrashes the cache.

A binary heap stored in a contiguous array uses the indexing rules
$\text{parent}(i) = (i-1)/2$, $\text{left}(i) = 2i+1$,
$\text{right}(i) = 2i+2$. Phase 1 builds the heap bottom-up by
sifting down each non-leaf node. Phase 2 swaps `s[0]` (the current
max) with `s[hi]`, shrinks the heap, and re-sifts the new root.

### `heap_sort_by`

    heap_sort_by[T](s: []T, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        val n = len(s)

        if n < 2 then return

Phase 1 — bottom-up max-heap construction. Start at the last
non-leaf node ($i = n/2 - 1$) and sift each one down.

        var i = n / 2 - 1

        while i >= 0
            sift_down(s, i, n, cmp)
            i--

Phase 2 — repeatedly swap the max into position `hi`, shrink the
heap, and re-sift.

        var hi = n - 1

        while hi > 0
            val tmp: T = s[0]
            s[0] = s[hi]
            s[hi] = tmp

            sift_down(s, 0, hi, cmp)
            hi--

### `sift_down`

Restore the max-heap invariant at `root`, given a heap of `size`
elements rooted at `s[0]`. Compares `s[root]` with the larger of its
two children; if a child is bigger, swap and continue down.

    sift_down[T](s: []T, root: int, size: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        var r = root

        while true
            var largest = r
            val left = 2 * r + 1
            val right = 2 * r + 2

            if left < size && cmp(s[left], s[largest]) > 0 then largest = left
            if right < size && cmp(s[right], s[largest]) > 0 then largest = right

            if largest == r then return

            val tmp: T = s[r]
            s[r] = s[largest]
            s[largest] = tmp
            r = largest


Quicksort
---------

Recursive partition-around-pivot. The classic divide-and-conquer
sort: average $O(n \log n)$ with low constants, but worst case
$O(n^2)$ when every pivot is the smallest or largest element of its
partition. Two well-known mitigations are applied here:

1. **Median-of-three pivot.** Sort `s[lo]`, `s[mid]`, `s[hi]` and use
   `s[mid]` as the pivot. This makes the worst case essentially
   impossible to hit by accident — sorted, reverse-sorted, and
   nearly-sorted inputs all become favourable.
2. **Insertion sort for small partitions.** Below 16 elements,
   insertion sort beats quicksort because of its lower constant
   factor and predictable cache behaviour.

### `quick_sort_by`

    quick_sort_by[T](s: []T, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        qsort_rec(s, 0, len(s) - 1, cmp)

    qsort_rec[T](s: []T, lo: int, hi: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        if hi - lo < 16
            insertion_sort_range(s, lo, hi, cmp)
            return

        val pivot = partition(s, lo, hi, cmp)

        qsort_rec(s, lo, pivot - 1, cmp)
        qsort_rec(s, pivot + 1, hi, cmp)


### `insertion_sort_range`

In-place insertion sort over the inclusive range `[lo, hi]`. $O(k^2)$
on a partition of size $k$, but with constants small enough that for
$k < 16$ it wins decisively over recursion overhead.

    insertion_sort_range[T](s: []T, lo: int, hi: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        for i in (lo + 1)..(hi)
            val key: T = s[i]
            var j = i - 1

            while j >= lo && cmp(s[j], key) > 0
                s[j + 1] = s[j]
                j--

            s[j + 1] = key


### `median_of_three`

Sorts the three values `s[lo]`, `s[mid]`, `s[hi]` in place. After the
call, `s[mid]` holds the median of the three. This is the cheapest
defence against adversarial inputs: it catches the sorted /
reverse-sorted / single-spike worst cases at the cost of three
comparisons and at most three swaps.

    median_of_three[T](s: []T, lo: int, mid: int, hi: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int) -> int
        if cmp(s[lo], s[mid]) > 0
            val tmp: T = s[lo]
            s[lo] = s[mid]
            s[mid] = tmp

        if cmp(s[lo], s[hi]) > 0
            val tmp: T = s[lo]
            s[lo] = s[hi]
            s[hi] = tmp

        if cmp(s[mid], s[hi]) > 0
            val tmp: T = s[mid]
            s[mid] = s[hi]
            s[hi] = tmp

        return mid


### `partition`

Hoare-style partition around a median-of-three pivot. The pivot is
swapped to position `hi - 1` (a "sentinel" slot), then `i` scans up
from `lo` for an element $\geq$ pivot and `j` scans down from `hi - 2`
for an element $\leq$ pivot; mismatched pairs are swapped. When the
pointers cross, the pivot is moved into position `i` — its final
sorted location — and `i` is returned.

    partition[T](s: []T, lo: int, hi: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int) -> int
        val mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2

        median_of_three(s, lo, mid, hi, cmp)

Move the median-of-three pivot to the sentinel slot `hi - 1`, just
inside the right boundary.

        val tmp_p: T = s[mid]
        s[mid] = s[hi - 1]
        s[hi - 1] = tmp_p
        val pivot: T = s[hi - 1]

        var i = lo
        var j = hi - 2

        while true
            while i <= j && cmp(s[i], pivot) < 0
                i++
            while j >= i && cmp(s[j], pivot) > 0
                j--

            if i >= j then break

            val tmp: T = s[i]
            s[i] = s[j]
            s[j] = tmp
            i++
            j--

Restore the pivot to its final position `i`. Everything to the left of
`i` is now $\leq$ pivot; everything to the right, $\geq$ pivot.

        val tmp_f: T = s[i]
        s[i] = s[hi - 1]
        s[hi - 1] = tmp_f

        return i


Introsort
---------

Introsort, due to David Musser (1997), wraps quicksort with a depth
limit. While the recursion stays shallow it behaves exactly like
quicksort — fast on the typical case. If the recursion ever exceeds
$2 \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor$ — the maximum sane depth for a balanced
divide-and-conquer — the algorithm bails out to heapsort on the
remaining range, guaranteeing $O(n \log n)$ even on inputs crafted to
hit quicksort's quadratic worst case.

### `intro_sort_by`

    intro_sort_by[T](s: []T, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        val n = len(s)

        if n < 2 then return

Compute the depth limit $2 \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor$ by counting how
many times $n$ can be halved before reaching 1.

        var depth = 0
        var v = n

        while v > 1
            depth++
            v = v / 2

        depth = depth * 2

        intro_rec(s, 0, n - 1, depth, cmp)

### `intro_rec`

The recursive worker. Three cases per call:

- Small partition ($k < 16$): switch to insertion sort.
- Depth budget exhausted: switch to heapsort on this range.
- Otherwise: median-of-three partition, recurse on both sides with
  `depth - 1`.

    intro_rec[T](s: []T, lo: int, hi: int, depth: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        if hi - lo < 16
            insertion_sort_range(s, lo, hi, cmp)
            return

        if depth == 0
            heap_sort_range(s, lo, hi, cmp)
            return

        val pivot = partition(s, lo, hi, cmp)

        intro_rec(s, lo, pivot - 1, depth - 1, cmp)
        intro_rec(s, pivot + 1, hi, depth - 1, cmp)


### `heap_sort_range`

Heapsort restricted to the inclusive subrange `s[lo..hi]`. Same
algorithm as `heap_sort_by`, but every index is offset by `lo` so the
heap lives in-place inside the parent quicksort partition.

    heap_sort_range[T](s: []T, lo: int, hi: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        val n = hi - lo + 1

        if n < 2 then return

Phase 1 — build the max-heap rooted at `s[lo]`.

        var i = n / 2 - 1

        while i >= 0
            sift_down_range(s, lo, i, n, cmp)
            i--

Phase 2 — repeatedly extract the max into the back of the range.

        var k = n - 1

        while k > 0
            val tmp: T = s[lo]
            s[lo] = s[lo + k]
            s[lo + k] = tmp

            sift_down_range(s, lo, 0, k, cmp)
            k--

    sift_down_range[T](s: []T, base: int, root: int, size: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        var r = root

        while true
            var largest = r
            val left = 2 * r + 1
            val right = 2 * r + 2

            if left < size && cmp(s[base + left], s[base + largest]) > 0 then largest = left
            if right < size && cmp(s[base + right], s[base + largest]) > 0 then largest = right

            if largest == r then return

            val tmp: T = s[base + r]
            s[base + r] = s[base + largest]
            s[base + largest] = tmp
            r = largest


Stable merge sort
-----------------

Bottom-up iterative merge sort — $O(n \log n)$ worst case, **stable**
(equal-key elements keep their original relative order), and free of
recursion. Requires $O(n)$ auxiliary memory for merging. The two
public entry points differ only in who allocates the buffer:

- `stable_sort_buf_by` takes a caller-supplied buffer (for embedded
  or no-allocator contexts).
- `stable_sort_by` allocates the buffer itself for callers in
  ordinary heap-using contexts.

### `stable_sort_buf_by`

The bottom-up loop iteratively merges adjacent runs of width
$1, 2, 4, 8, \ldots$ until one run covers the whole array. After the
$k$-th outer iteration, every $2^k$-element block is sorted; the
$(k{+}1)$-st iteration merges adjacent blocks pairwise.

    stable_sort_buf_by[T](s: []T, buf: []T, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        val n = len(s)

        if n < 2 then return

        var width = 1

        while width < n
            var lo = 0

            while lo < n
                val mid = lo + width
                var hi = lo + width * 2

                if hi > n then hi = n
                if mid >= n
                    lo += width * 2
                else
                    merge(s, buf, lo, mid, hi, cmp)
                    lo += width * 2

            width = width * 2

### `merge`

Merge two sorted adjacent runs `s[lo..mid)` and `s[mid..hi)` in place,
using `buf` as scratch. Stability comes from the `<=` comparison on
the left run: ties always take from the left first.

    merge[T](s: []T, buf: []T, lo: int, mid: int, hi: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int)

Snapshot the merge region into `buf`. After this point we can
overwrite `s[lo..hi)` freely while reading from `buf`.

        for i in lo..<hi
            buf[i] = s[i]

        var i = lo
        var j = mid
        var k = lo

        while i < mid && j < hi
            if cmp(buf[i], buf[j]) <= 0
                s[k] = buf[i]
                i++
            else
                s[k] = buf[j]
                j++
            k++

When the right run is exhausted first, the left run's tail still
needs to be drained from the buffer. The right run's leftover (if
any) is **already in place** in `s` — we never overwrote it past `j`,
and the merge region was fully snapshot-then-rewritten only up to `k`.

        while i < mid
            s[k] = buf[i]
            i++
            k++


### `stable_sort_by`

Convenience wrapper: allocates a fresh `[n]T` buffer and forwards to
`stable_sort_buf_by`. Use this when allocation is acceptable;
otherwise pass your own buffer to `stable_sort_buf_by`.

    stable_sort_by[T](s: []T, cmp: (T, T) -> int)
        val n = len(s)

        if n < 2 then return

        val buf = (new [n]T)[:]

        stable_sort_buf_by(s, buf, cmp)


`is_sorted` — sortedness check
------------------------------

`is_sorted(s, cmp)` returns `true` iff `s` is non-decreasing
according to `cmp`. Useful as a post-condition in tests and as a
cheap "did sort actually do anything" sanity check.

    is_sorted[T](s: []T, cmp: (T, T) -> int) -> bool
        val n = len(s)

        for i in 1..<n
            if cmp(s[i - 1], s[i]) > 0 then return false

        true


Convenience wrappers
--------------------

### `cmp_int` / `cmp_i64`

Three-way comparators for the two common signed integer types.
Branchless `a - b` would overflow on extreme values, so this uses an
explicit branch.

    cmp_int(a: int, b: int) -> int
        if a < b then -1
        else if a > b then 1
        else 0

    cmp_i64(a: i64, b: i64) -> int
        if a < b then -1
        else if a > b then 1
        else 0


### `sort_int` / `sort_i64`

Type-specialized aliases for the most common sorts.

    sort_int(s: []int) = sort_by(s, cmp_int)

    sort_i64(s: []i64) = sort_by(s, cmp_i64)


### `stable_sort_int` / `stable_sort_i64`

Stable variants of the integer sorts.

    stable_sort_int(s: []int) = stable_sort_by(s, cmp_int)

    stable_sort_i64(s: []i64) = stable_sort_by(s, cmp_i64)

Use `is_sorted(s, cmp_int)` or `is_sorted(s, cmp_i64)` to check
ordering after sorting.


### `sort_int_desc` — descending sort for `[]int`

A dedicated descending sort. Without closures-with-captures it isn't
possible to write a generic "negate this comparator" wrapper, so
descending sort gets its own copy of the heapsort core with the
comparison flipped. (The "max-heap with reversed compare" is
arithmetically a min-heap, which extracted into the back of the array
yields a descending sort.)

    sort_int_desc(s: []int)
        val n = len(s)

        if n < 2 then return

        var i = n / 2 - 1

        while i >= 0
            sift_down_desc_int(s, i, n)
            i--

        var hi = n - 1

        while hi > 0
            val tmp = s[0]
            s[0] = s[hi]
            s[hi] = tmp

            sift_down_desc_int(s, 0, hi)
            hi--

    sift_down_desc_int(s: []int, root: int, size: int)
        var r = root

        while true
            var smallest = r
            val left = 2 * r + 1
            val right = 2 * r + 2

            if left < size && s[left] < s[smallest] then smallest = left
            if right < size && s[right] < s[smallest] then smallest = right

            if smallest == r then return

            val tmp = s[r]
            s[r] = s[smallest]
            s[smallest] = tmp
            r = smallest


Tests
=====

The tests cover each algorithm independently. Common cases exercised
across all sorts: basic random input, already-sorted, reverse-sorted,
duplicates, single-element, empty, all-same, large random.

### `sort_int` — the default int sort

    #test
    test_sort_int_basic() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 5
        s[1] = 3
        s[2] = 1
        s[3] = 4
        s[4] = 2

        sort_int(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 1, "0")
        assert(s[1] == 2, "1")
        assert(s[2] == 3, "2")
        assert(s[3] == 4, "3")
        assert(s[4] == 5, "4")

    #test
    test_sort_int_already_sorted() -> unit
        val s = new [4]int
        s[0] = 1
        s[1] = 2
        s[2] = 3
        s[3] = 4

        sort_int(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 1, "0")
        assert(s[3] == 4, "3")

    #test
    test_sort_int_reverse() -> unit
        val s = new [4]int
        s[0] = 4
        s[1] = 3
        s[2] = 2
        s[3] = 1

        sort_int(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 1, "0")
        assert(s[3] == 4, "3")

    #test
    test_sort_int_duplicates() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 3
        s[1] = 1
        s[2] = 3
        s[3] = 1
        s[4] = 2

        sort_int(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 1, "0")
        assert(s[1] == 1, "1")
        assert(s[2] == 2, "2")
        assert(s[3] == 3, "3")
        assert(s[4] == 3, "4")

    #test
    test_sort_int_single() -> unit
        val s = new [1]int
        s[0] = 42

        sort_int(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 42, "unchanged")

    #test
    test_sort_int_empty() -> unit
        val s: []int = []
        sort_int(s)

    #test
    test_sort_int_negative() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 3
        s[1] = -1
        s[2] = 0
        s[3] = -5
        s[4] = 2

        sort_int(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == -5, "0")
        assert(s[1] == -1, "1")
        assert(s[2] == 0, "2")
        assert(s[3] == 2, "3")
        assert(s[4] == 3, "4")

    #test
    test_sort_int_all_same() -> unit
        val s = new [4]int
        s[0] = 7
        s[1] = 7
        s[2] = 7
        s[3] = 7

        sort_int(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 7, "0")
        assert(s[3] == 7, "3")


### `sort_int_desc`

    #test
    test_sort_int_desc() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 1
        s[1] = 5
        s[2] = 3
        s[3] = 2
        s[4] = 4

        sort_int_desc(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 5, "0")
        assert(s[1] == 4, "1")
        assert(s[2] == 3, "2")
        assert(s[3] == 2, "3")
        assert(s[4] == 1, "4")


### `is_sorted`

    #test
    test_is_sorted_yes() -> unit
        val s = new [4]int
        s[0] = 1
        s[1] = 2
        s[2] = 3
        s[3] = 4

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")

    #test
    test_is_sorted_no() -> unit
        val s = new [3]int
        s[0] = 1
        s[1] = 3
        s[2] = 2

        assert(!is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "not sorted")

    #test
    test_is_sorted_empty() -> unit
        val s: []int = []
        assert(is_sorted(s, cmp_int), "empty is sorted")

    #test
    test_is_sorted_single() -> unit
        val s = new [1]int
        s[0] = 42
        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "single is sorted")


### Sort then verify with `is_sorted`

A round-trip check: the array is unsorted before the call and sorted
after, with the right min and max in their final positions.

    #test
    test_sort_then_check() -> unit
        val s = new [8]int
        s[0] = 42
        s[1] = 7
        s[2] = 99
        s[3] = 1
        s[4] = 55
        s[5] = 3
        s[6] = 88
        s[7] = 12

        assert(!is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "initially unsorted")
        sort_int(s[:])
        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted after sort")
        assert(s[0] == 1, "min")
        assert(s[7] == 99, "max")


### `sort_by` with a custom comparator

Verifies that user-supplied comparators dispatch correctly through
the generic `sort_by` entry point. `cmp_desc` reverses the order.

    cmp_desc(a: int, b: int) -> int = b - a

    #test
    test_sort_by_custom_comparator() -> unit
        val s = new [4]int
        s[0] = 1
        s[1] = 4
        s[2] = 2
        s[3] = 3

        sort_by(s[:], cmp_desc)

        assert(s[0] == 4, "0")
        assert(s[1] == 3, "1")
        assert(s[2] == 2, "2")
        assert(s[3] == 1, "3")


### `sort_i64`

    #test
    test_sort_i64() -> unit
        val s = new [4]i64
        s[0] = 100i64
        s[1] = 1i64
        s[2] = 50i64
        s[3] = 10i64

        sort_i64(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 1i64, "0")
        assert(s[1] == 10i64, "1")
        assert(s[2] == 50i64, "2")
        assert(s[3] == 100i64, "3")


### Quicksort tests

Same suite of cases run directly against `quick_sort_by`, including
already-sorted (a worst-case input for naive quicksort, but tractable
under median-of-three) and a 20-element shuffle that exercises the
recursion.

    #test
    test_quick_sort_basic() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 5
        s[1] = 3
        s[2] = 1
        s[3] = 4
        s[4] = 2

        quick_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(s[0] == 1, "0")
        assert(s[1] == 2, "1")
        assert(s[2] == 3, "2")
        assert(s[3] == 4, "3")
        assert(s[4] == 5, "4")

    #test
    test_quick_sort_already_sorted() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 1
        s[1] = 2
        s[2] = 3
        s[3] = 4
        s[4] = 5

        quick_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "still sorted")

    #test
    test_quick_sort_reverse() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 5
        s[1] = 4
        s[2] = 3
        s[3] = 2
        s[4] = 1

        quick_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")
        assert(s[0] == 1, "min")
        assert(s[4] == 5, "max")

    #test
    test_quick_sort_duplicates() -> unit
        val s = new [7]int
        s[0] = 3
        s[1] = 1
        s[2] = 4
        s[3] = 1
        s[4] = 5
        s[5] = 9
        s[6] = 2

        quick_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")
        assert(s[0] == 1, "min")
        assert(s[6] == 9, "max")

    #test
    test_quick_sort_small() -> unit
        val s = new [2]int
        s[0] = 2
        s[1] = 1

        quick_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(s[0] == 1, "0")
        assert(s[1] == 2, "1")

    #test
    test_quick_sort_single() -> unit
        val s = new [1]int
        s[0] = 42

        quick_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(s[0] == 42, "unchanged")

    #test
    test_quick_sort_empty() -> unit
        val s: []int = []
        quick_sort_by(s, cmp_int)

    #test
    test_quick_sort_large() -> unit
        val s = new [20]int
        s[0] = 19
        s[1] = 7
        s[2] = 15
        s[3] = 3
        s[4] = 11
        s[5] = 1
        s[6] = 18
        s[7] = 8
        s[8] = 14
        s[9] = 4
        s[10] = 12
        s[11] = 0
        s[12] = 17
        s[13] = 6
        s[14] = 13
        s[15] = 2
        s[16] = 16
        s[17] = 9
        s[18] = 10
        s[19] = 5

        quick_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")
        assert(s[0] == 0, "min")
        assert(s[19] == 19, "max")


### Introsort tests

`sort_by` is the public face; `intro_sort_by` is the implementation
underneath. The first test confirms `intro_sort_by` directly; the
`sort_by` test verifies the delegation; the `heap_sort_by` test
confirms the heapsort entry point still works on its own.

    #test
    test_intro_sort_basic() -> unit
        val s = new [8]int
        s[0] = 42
        s[1] = 7
        s[2] = 99
        s[3] = 1
        s[4] = 55
        s[5] = 3
        s[6] = 88
        s[7] = 12

        intro_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")
        assert(s[0] == 1, "min")
        assert(s[7] == 99, "max")

    #test
    test_intro_sort_all_same() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 7
        s[1] = 7
        s[2] = 7
        s[3] = 7
        s[4] = 7

        intro_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(s[0] == 7, "0")
        assert(s[4] == 7, "4")

    #test
    test_sort_by_is_introsort() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 5
        s[1] = 3
        s[2] = 1
        s[3] = 4
        s[4] = 2

        sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted via introsort")

    #test
    test_heap_sort_by() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 5
        s[1] = 3
        s[2] = 1
        s[3] = 4
        s[4] = 2

        heap_sort_by(s[:], cmp_int)

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted via heapsort")


### Stable sort tests

Same case grid as quicksort, plus a check of the no-allocator
buffered variant.

    #test
    test_stable_sort_basic() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 5
        s[1] = 3
        s[2] = 1
        s[3] = 4
        s[4] = 2

        stable_sort_int(s[:])

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")
        assert(s[0] == 1, "min")
        assert(s[4] == 5, "max")

    #test
    test_stable_sort_already_sorted() -> unit
        val s = new [4]int
        s[0] = 1
        s[1] = 2
        s[2] = 3
        s[3] = 4

        stable_sort_int(s[:])

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "still sorted")

    #test
    test_stable_sort_reverse() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 5
        s[1] = 4
        s[2] = 3
        s[3] = 2
        s[4] = 1

        stable_sort_int(s[:])

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")

    #test
    test_stable_sort_duplicates() -> unit
        val s = new [6]int
        s[0] = 3
        s[1] = 1
        s[2] = 4
        s[3] = 1
        s[4] = 5
        s[5] = 9

        stable_sort_int(s[:])

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")
        assert(s[0] == 1, "min")
        assert(s[5] == 9, "max")

    #test
    test_stable_sort_single() -> unit
        val s = new [1]int
        s[0] = 42

        stable_sort_int(s[:])

        assert(s[0] == 42, "unchanged")

    #test
    test_stable_sort_empty() -> unit
        val s: []int = []
        stable_sort_int(s)

The non-allocating variant takes a caller-provided buffer of at
least `len(s)` elements.

    #test
    test_stable_sort_buf() -> unit
        val s = new [5]int
        s[0] = 5
        s[1] = 3
        s[2] = 1
        s[3] = 4
        s[4] = 2

        val buf = new [5]int

        stable_sort_buf_by(s[:], buf[:], cmp_int)

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")

    #test
    test_stable_sort_large() -> unit
        val s = new [20]int
        s[0] = 19
        s[1] = 7
        s[2] = 15
        s[3] = 3
        s[4] = 11
        s[5] = 1
        s[6] = 18
        s[7] = 8
        s[8] = 14
        s[9] = 4
        s[10] = 12
        s[11] = 0
        s[12] = 17
        s[13] = 6
        s[14] = 13
        s[15] = 2
        s[16] = 16
        s[17] = 9
        s[18] = 10
        s[19] = 5

        stable_sort_int(s[:])

        assert(is_sorted(s[:], cmp_int), "sorted")
        assert(s[0] == 0, "min")
        assert(s[19] == 19, "max")


Design notes
------------

**Introsort as the default.** `sort_by` delegates to `intro_sort_by`:
quicksort with median-of-three partitioning and insertion-sort
cutoffs, falling back to heapsort once recursion depth exceeds
$2 \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor$. This combination retains quicksort's
average performance while preserving heapsort's $O(n \log n)$
worst-case guarantee — the standard pick for general-purpose
comparison sorts since Musser's 1997 paper.

**Three top-level algorithms.** `quick_sort_by` for raw average
speed (when the input distribution is friendly), `heap_sort_by` for
guaranteed worst-case complexity with no recursion (real-time /
embedded), `intro_sort_by` (the default via `sort_by`) for the best
of both.

**Insertion-sort cutoff at 16.** Below this size, insertion sort's
low constants and predictable cache pattern beat divide-and-conquer.
The cutoff matches the value used in `libstdc++` and Go's `sort`.

**Median-of-three pivot.** Sorting `s[lo]`, `s[mid]`, `s[hi]` and
using the median as pivot defeats the canonical adversarial inputs
(sorted, reverse-sorted, single-spike) at the cost of three
comparisons per partition.

**Stable merge sort is bottom-up.** Iterative — no recursion stack —
and runs in $O(n \log n)$. Stability comes from the `<=` tie-break in
the merge step, which always prefers the left run on equality. The
buffered variant lets embedded callers avoid heap allocation by
supplying their own scratch space.
